well joining me now is chief executive  of the royal meteorological society liz  bentley and let's just remind us what  does an amber warning mean  yeah so it's basically to uh to warn us  of the heat that's coming and there's a  heat health warning as well that's also  been issued and it's about taking action  now over the next few days as  temperatures start to rise  and peak probably around friday i think  into the mid 30s but over the course of  the next few days temperatures reaching  those heatwave categories so getting  into the high 20s and the low 30s and  then peaking around the mid 30s on  friday  and not just daytime temperatures  getting to get into those high levels  but also nighttime temperatures will be  staying up and we could see one or two  tropical nights over the course of the  next few days and that's where the  temperature doesn't drop below 20  degrees celsius so we need to take  action to to be able to look after  vulnerable people the elderly who  struggle a little bit more when we get  these heat events but also for for you  know generally healthy human beings just  to take extra precautions because of the  heat so taking on extra water keeping  hydrated and keeping out of the heat as  best you can  now we had high temperatures in july and  we understand that these temperatures  aren't going to be as high as this does  this therefore mean it's not as serious  yeah so we we saw over 40 degrees at the  end of july and i don't suspect during  this heatwave event that we'll see  temperatures reaching 40 degrees  this week  but it's a more prolonged period so if  you remember at the end of july probably  was about a 48-hour period where we had  the intense heat this is lasting for for  a longer period of about five or six  days uh until the heat  warning is in place now until  sunday this week  and so it's that prolonged period that  actually can be  more damaging really to our health  because it puts extra strain on our body  day and night day and night  consecutively day after day and that's  really where the health problems start  to come because our bodies don't have  time to rest and recuperate you know the  high temperatures mean it's working very  hard to keep us cool both day and night  and when we put that extra strain on it  on our bodies day after day that's where  we're going to start to see some  problems people you know struggling with  heat exhaustion  we often see an increase in mortality  rate when we get temperatures this high  as well  and and in terms of of what's happening  after this do we expect uh i mean i know  that there's a limit to how far ahead  you're able to predict but are we  expecting more spells of hot summer hot  weather over the summer or  or is the feeling that this could be the  end of it  so just looking at the forecast ahead i  think you know that the high  temperatures the really extreme heat  that lead to these heat wave warnings  there's nothing in the forecast yet as  we go through august it's a bit too  early to to really kind of see the  detail  but i would say because of climate  change we are you know seeing more  heatwave events they're becoming more  frequent when they happen they tend to  be more prolonged and we get these  intense heat events the kind of  record-breaking events that we saw at  the end of july so these are happening  more often so whether we see more this  year or in future years as i say the  likelihood is they're just going to  increase in in frequency and intensity  as we go forward in time  the fact that we are seeing these  extreme periods of different weather be  it very hot weather wet weather does it  tell us anything about what what's going  to come ahead in the autumn and in the  winter did the fact that it has been so  hot this summer doesn't mean it will be  a milder winter or or or could it be the  opposite  yeah so we don't tend to have these  linkages to what the weather's doing in  one season as to how it's going to  change as we move into the next season  we do have seasonal forecasts we have  longer range forecasts that give us that  extra detail of trends of whether things  are going to be above or below average  whether that's temperature or rainfall  and then we obviously have our climate  projections that look decades or you  know a century ahead and it's the  climate projections that are really  giving us that steer to the fact that  you know if if we look forward in time  we're going to see warmer wetter winters  more likely in future hotter drier  summers as we go forward in time and  we've already seen that starting to play  out in our changing climate already but  it's going to you know  lead to a kind of longer longer trend as  we go forward as well  uh and and in terms of that longer trend  that is the pattern is it that we're  just going to have to get used to  absolutely so things that we see as  extreme now the temperature of 40  degrees at the end of july and 36 at the  end of the this week if you think we've  only recorded 36 degrees in this country  13 times and 10 of those happened since  the turn of the century since 2000  they're becoming more frequent events  but we still see them as extreme if we  go forward a few decades 36 degrees or  even 40 degrees that we saw at the end  of july are going to become much more  normal temperatures that we see in the  summer not seen as extreme events as  we'd still class them today  okay  good a good chance to remind everybody  to keep  out of the sun if you possibly can and  as you say keep hydrated and try and  avoid getting too much of it liz bentley  from the royal meteorological society  thank you very much thank you
 

